% ============================================================================
% hormone-glands — each hormone and the endocrine gland that secretes it
% (adj-facts-stdlib, BIOLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% The first big map of endocrinology, and a rung a MEDICINE agent reasons UP
% from: the body's chemical messengers (hormones) are each MADE by a particular
% endocrine gland, and the classic middle/high-school recall is "which gland
% makes hormone X?" Insulin comes from the pancreas, thyroxine from the thyroid,
% cortisol from the adrenal glands, melatonin from the pineal gland, growth
% hormone from the pituitary. Before you can reason about a hormone excess, a
% deficiency, or a gland tumor you must first know WHICH gland the hormone comes
% from. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? hormone_gland(insulin, $Gland)      % pancreas
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `hormone_gland(hormone, gland)` carrying the table's citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the gland
% WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on a word
% that is not one of these grounded hormones (it never invents a gland).
%
% Each grounded hormone and the gland the source says makes it, as a little
% truth table (several hormones map to the SAME gland — that is expected):
%
%     hormone              gland               a beginner's cue
%     ------------------   -----------------   ------------------------------
%     insulin              pancreas            beta cells, lowers blood sugar
%     glucagon             pancreas            alpha cells, raises blood sugar
%     thyroxine            thyroid             the metabolism hormone (T4)
%     calcitonin           thyroid             parafollicular ("C") cells
%     cortisol             adrenal_gland       the "stress" glucocorticoid
%     epinephrine          adrenal_gland       adrenaline, adrenal medulla
%     growth_hormone       pituitary           makes bones and muscles grow
%     melatonin            pineal_gland        the sleep / daily-cycle hormone
%     parathyroid_hormone  parathyroid_gland   raises blood calcium
%     testosterone         testis              the principal androgen
%     estrogen             ovary               a female sex hormone
%     progesterone         ovary               a female sex hormone
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a gland and recall a hormone it makes:
%
%     ? hormone_gland($H, pituitary)        % growth_hormone
%
% A word that is NOT one of these grounded hormones — `aldosterone`, `renin`, a
% neurotransmitter, a random word — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall on it
% ABSTAINS rather than inventing a gland. (`aldosterone` is a real adrenal
% hormone, but the fetched span that names it did not also name the adrenal gland
% in the same sentence, so it was DROPPED rather than grounded on memory — that
% is the provenance discipline this table is built to demonstrate.) That
% honest-abstention property is what makes the table safe to build a reasoner on:
% it answers exactly the hormone→gland pairs it can ground, and only those.
%
% The `gland` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom naming the endocrine
% gland the source EXPLICITLY states makes that hormone — never one the source
% does not support. (Where several hormones share a gland, the atom is repeated:
% `pancreas` appears for both insulin and glucagon, `ovary` for both estrogen and
% progesterone.)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance. All 12 pairs come from primary U.S. government references — the
% NCI SEER Training Modules (National Cancer Institute) and MedlinePlus
% (National Library of Medicine / NIH) — so `trust authoritative`.
%
% EVERY ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SPAN AND ITS OWN LOCATOR, below, in the row's
% `{ ... }` block (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e). This is a CROSS-PAGE conversion: the
% twelve rows are grounded on six different pages (SEER pancreas, thyroid,
% adrenal, pituitary and gonads, plus MedlinePlus cortisol-test and
% growth-hormone-tests), so a row's locator is part of its warrant, not a
% detail inherited from the table.
%
% This header used to re-quote all 12 spans a second time. They now ship in the
% rows; a second copy is a second thing to keep true, and in #15137 two of five
% such restatements had drifted from their page while sitting under the words
% "quoted word-for-word".
%
% WHAT #14986 CHANGED. The table had ONE envelope, and it was the INSULIN row's
% own sentence — so recalling progesterone returned a warrant about beta cells.
% The envelope now carries the FRAMING span (hormones are secreted by endocrine
% glands), which warrants no row.
%
% THREE SPANS ARE WIDENED, per ../README.md, "A citation must name its own
% subject" — widen the quote until it is self-contained:
%
%   thyroxine       its sentence says "the gland" and names none
%   melatonin       its sentence credits "the pinealocytes" and names no gland
%   growth_hormone  its sentence calls the hormone only "GH"
%
%   Each is widened back to the nearest sentence that supplies the missing
%   name, and no further. The reason is recorded on the row itself.
%
% TWO ROWS THE PAGES DO NOT SPELL THE WAY THIS TABLE DOES:
%
%   glucagon        the SEER page writes "glucagons"; the span is quoted as the
%                   page has it, and the row atom stays `glucagon`
%   melatonin       cited from pituitary.html, because that SEER page covers the
%                   pituitary AND pineal glands
%
% `estrogen` and `progesterone` share one sentence, which states both. Each row
% carries its own copy so either can be re-checked, or changed, alone.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts, feedback_no_byte_arithmetic_for_llm.)
% ============================================================================

table hormone_gland {
    columns hormone, gland

    row (insulin, pancreas) {
        source "Beta cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone insulin in response to a high concentration of glucose in the blood."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/pancreas.html"
    }
    row (glucagon, pancreas) {
        % The page spells it "glucagons"; the hormone is glucagon.
        % The span is quoted as the page has it -- correcting a quote
        % silently is how a citation stops being checkable.
        source "Alpha cells in the pancreatic islets secrete the hormone glucagons in response to a low concentration of glucose in the blood."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/pancreas.html"
    }
    row (thyroxine, thyroid) {
        % WIDENED. The sentence that states thyroxine says "the
        % gland" and names none; quoted alone it grounds nothing.
        % Widened to the paragraph's first sentence -- the nearest one
        % naming the thyroid, since neither sentence between them
        % does. README, "A citation must name its own subject".
        source "The thyroid gland is a very vascular organ that is located in the neck. It consists of two lobes, one on each side of the trachea, just below the larynx or voice box. The two lobes are connected by a narrow band of tissue called the isthmus. Internally, the gland consists of follicles, which produce thyroxine and triiodothyronine hormones."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/thyroid.html"
    }
    row (calcitonin, thyroid) {
        source "Calcitonin is secreted by the parafollicular cells of the thyroid gland."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/thyroid.html"
    }
    row (cortisol, adrenal_gland) {
        source "Cortisol is a hormone made by your adrenal glands, two small glands that sit above your kidneys."
        locator "https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/cortisol-test/"
    }
    row (epinephrine, adrenal_gland) {
        source "The adrenal medulla develops from neural tissue and secretes two hormones, epinephrine and norepinephrine."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/adrenal.html"
    }
    row (growth_hormone, pituitary) {
        % WIDENED. The sentence placing GH in the pituitary calls it
        % only GH, which is not this row's subject spelled out. A
        % first draft shipped it anyway with a comment claiming the
        % span defined GH -- it did not. Widened back to the sentence
        % that expands it, so the span names the hormone AND the
        % gland without a reader supplying either.
        source "GH, also known as human growth hormone, controls your body's growth. It also helps control metabolism, the process your body uses to make energy from the food you eat. GH is made in the pituitary gland, a small organ at the base of your brain that controls many functions, including growth."
        locator "https://medlineplus.gov/lab-tests/growth-hormone-tests/"
    }
    row (melatonin, pineal_gland) {
        % WIDENED. The melatonin sentence credits the pinealocytes
        % and names no gland; a reader has to already know whose
        % cells those are. The sentence before it says so outright,
        % so the span now carries both. NOTE the locator: this SEER
        % page covers the pituitary AND pineal glands, so a pineal
        % row legitimately cites pituitary.html.
        source "The pineal gland consists of portions of neurons, neuroglial cells, and specialized secretory cells called pinealocytes. The pinealocytes synthesize the hormone melatonin and secrete it directly into the cerebrospinal fluid, which takes it into the blood."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/pituitary.html"
    }
    row (parathyroid_hormone, parathyroid_gland) {
        source "These are parathyroid glands, and they secrete parathyroid hormone or parathormone."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/thyroid.html"
    }
    row (testosterone, testis) {
        source "The principal androgen is testosterone, which is secreted by the testes."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/gonads.html"
    }
    row (estrogen, ovary) {
        source "Two groups of female sex hormones are produced in the ovaries, the estrogens and progesterone."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/gonads.html"
    }
    row (progesterone, ovary) {
        % Shares estrogen's span: one sentence states both. Each row
        % carries its own copy so either can be checked, or changed,
        % without the other.
        source "Two groups of female sex hormones are produced in the ovaries, the estrogens and progesterone."
        locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/gonads.html"
    }

    % The envelope is the FRAMING span — what defends the TABLE, not any one
    % row: that hormones are secreted by endocrine glands at all. Until #14986
    % it was the INSULIN row's own sentence, so recalling progesterone came
    % back warranted by a sentence about beta cells.
    source "The endocrine system is made up of the endocrine glands that secrete hormones."
    locator "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/anatomy/endocrine/glands/"
    trust authoritative
}
